  <section class="prose" aria-labelledby="success">
    <h2 id="success">Where students go</h2>

    <p>Students I have taught, coached, or mentored have gone on to a position in a United States Navy fleet band, the Chautauqua summer instrumental program, and a touring position with Tim Zimmerman and the King&rsquo;s Brass. Others have continued to undergraduate and graduate study at the Eastman School of Music, Rice University, Carnegie Mellon University, and DePaul University. An earlier student received a merit-based music scholarship to Harvard University.</p>

    <h2>In lessons</h2>

    <p>We start from the sound you want and work backward to the physical action that produces it &mdash; most problems that feel technical turn out to be problems of concept first. I want you to understand why a musical choice works rather than to copy mine, so you leave each lesson able to diagnose your own playing and with a practice plan rather than a list of corrections.</p>

    <p>Students arrive preparing for different careers, and I treat all of them as legitimate. Orchestral auditions, graduate study, military bands, teaching, chamber music, and portfolio careers each ask for different preparation, and a studio that prepares only for one of them is preparing most of its students for something they are not going to do.</p>

    <h2>Beyond the universities</h2>

    <p>I also teach privately and have worked with students through the Academy of Music at the University of Northwestern, the Greater Twin Cities Youth Symphonies, the Mannes Preparatory Division, Boston College High School, and at Chautauqua &mdash; elementary through adult, majors and non-majors, in lessons, sectionals, masterclasses, chamber coaching, and audition preparation. For three years I was the sole instructor of record for Brass Methods at the University of Minnesota, teaching trumpet, horn, trombone, euphonium, and tuba to future music educators.</p>
  </section>

